1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,773
This web page has twenty four books, spread over three pages.

2
00:00:03,773 --> 00:00:07,470
Copying them into a spreadsheet by hand would take half an hour.

3
00:00:07,470 --> 00:00:14,864
This Python script visits every page, reads every title, price and rating, and saves them all to a file, in about three seconds.

4
00:00:14,864 --> 00:00:16,770
Today you'll write it, from zero.

5
00:00:17,470 --> 00:00:18,702
This is web scraping.

6
00:00:18,702 --> 00:00:23,866
A program that reads a web page the way you do, and copies out the parts you care about.

7
00:00:23,866 --> 00:00:27,035
Prices, lists, tables, anything you can see on a page.

8
00:00:27,035 --> 00:00:32,199
And I'll show you the three errors every beginner hits, with the real message on screen.

9
00:00:32,899 --> 00:00:35,811
First, a quick word about doing this the right way.

10
00:00:35,811 --> 00:00:37,239
Only scrape public pages.

11
00:00:37,239 --> 00:00:41,693
Read the site's terms, and its robots dot t x t file, which we'll check later.

12
00:00:41,693 --> 00:00:43,292
Go slow, one page at a time.

13
00:00:43,292 --> 00:00:45,120
And never collect personal data.

14
00:00:45,120 --> 00:00:51,516
To keep things safe, every example today runs against a small practice shop I built, running on my own computer.

15
00:00:51,516 --> 00:00:53,686
That's why the address says localhost.

16
00:00:54,386 --> 00:00:55,434
Here's the plan.

17
00:00:55,434 --> 00:00:57,530
Download the page with requests.

18
00:00:57,530 --> 00:01:01,655
Turn the H T M L into something searchable with Beautiful Soup.

19
00:01:01,655 --> 00:01:04,603
Find the parts you want with C S S selectors.

20
00:01:04,603 --> 00:01:05,978
Loop over every page.

21
00:01:05,978 --> 00:01:09,776
And save everything to a C S V file you can open in Excel.

22
00:01:10,476 --> 00:01:13,125
You need Python and three free libraries.

23
00:01:13,125 --> 00:01:17,519
In a terminal, type pip install requests beautiful soup four pandas.

24
00:01:17,519 --> 00:01:19,135
Requests downloads pages.

25
00:01:19,135 --> 00:01:23,076
Beautiful soup four, imported as b s four, reads the H T M L.

26
00:01:23,076 --> 00:01:24,821
And pandas saves the table.

27
00:01:24,821 --> 00:01:27,082
This prints the versions I'm using.

28
00:01:27,782 --> 00:01:28,318
Step one.

29
00:01:28,318 --> 00:01:29,270
Import requests.

30
00:01:29,270 --> 00:01:33,316
Put the address in a variable called url, and call requests dot get.

31
00:01:33,316 --> 00:01:38,731
Always add timeout equals ten, so your script never waits forever if a site doesn't answer.

32
00:01:38,731 --> 00:01:40,159
Then print three things.

33
00:01:40,159 --> 00:01:44,324
The status code, how long the text is, and the first characters of it.

34
00:01:45,024 --> 00:01:45,427
Run it.

35
00:01:45,427 --> 00:01:46,117
Two hundred.

36
00:01:46,117 --> 00:01:48,532
That's the code for O K, the page arrived.

37
00:01:48,532 --> 00:01:51,292
Three thousand eight hundred and ten characters.

38
00:01:51,292 --> 00:01:52,845
And here's the start of it.

39
00:01:52,845 --> 00:01:57,790
That's the raw H T M L, the same text your browser receives, before it draws the page.

40
00:01:58,490 --> 00:02:00,821
But how do we find a price in all that text?

41
00:02:00,821 --> 00:02:04,530
Open the page in your browser, right click a book, and choose Inspect.

42
00:02:04,530 --> 00:02:07,496
The developer tools show the H T M L of that exact book.

43
00:02:07,496 --> 00:02:10,092
Every book is an article tag with the class book.

44
00:02:10,092 --> 00:02:13,218
Inside it, the title is in an h three with the class title.

45
00:02:13,218 --> 00:02:15,655
The price is a paragraph with the class price.

46
00:02:15,655 --> 00:02:17,880
And the rating has a data stars attribute.

47
00:02:17,880 --> 00:02:19,469
Those class names are our map.

48
00:02:20,169 --> 00:02:21,259
Now Beautiful Soup.

49
00:02:21,259 --> 00:02:23,382
From b s four, import Beautiful Soup.

50
00:02:23,382 --> 00:02:28,832
Pass it the page text, and the name of the parser, h t m l dot parser, which comes with Python.

51
00:02:28,832 --> 00:02:32,102
The result is called soup, and it lets you ask questions.

52
00:02:32,102 --> 00:02:34,167
Soup dot title gives the page title.

53
00:02:34,167 --> 00:02:37,035
Soup dot find h two gives the first h two heading.

54
00:02:37,035 --> 00:02:40,018
And get text keeps just the words, without the tags.

55
00:02:40,718 --> 00:02:41,157
Run it.

56
00:02:41,157 --> 00:02:44,421
The page title, Pine Street Books, catalog page one.

57
00:02:44,421 --> 00:02:47,307
And the heading, all books, page one of three.

58
00:02:47,307 --> 00:02:48,876
Python can read the page.

59
00:02:49,576 --> 00:02:51,420
Step three, the important one.

60
00:02:51,420 --> 00:02:56,277
Soup dot select takes a C S S selector, and returns every element that matches.

61
00:02:56,277 --> 00:02:59,843
Article dot book means an article tag with the class book.

62
00:02:59,843 --> 00:03:01,072
Watch the real page.

63
00:03:01,072 --> 00:03:03,900
All eight books light up, one after the other.

64
00:03:03,900 --> 00:03:07,036
Python now has a list of eight boxes, one per book.

65
00:03:07,735 --> 00:03:09,538
Then we look inside each box.

66
00:03:09,538 --> 00:03:13,144
Select one returns the first match, only inside that book.

67
00:03:13,144 --> 00:03:16,191
Dot title for the title, dot price for the price.

68
00:03:16,191 --> 00:03:21,662
And get text with strip equals true removes the spaces and line breaks around the words.

69
00:03:21,662 --> 00:03:24,832
Without it, you get messy text full of blank space.

70
00:03:25,532 --> 00:03:29,926
Here's the price selector, running on the real page, one book at a time.

71
00:03:29,926 --> 00:03:35,845
Each yellow outline is the element dot price, and on the right, the text Python pulled out of it.

72
00:03:36,545 --> 00:03:37,854
And the real output.

73
00:03:37,854 --> 00:03:41,324
Eight books found, and every title next to its price.

74
00:03:41,324 --> 00:03:42,698
That's a web scraper.

75
00:03:42,698 --> 00:03:43,615
Fifteen lines.

76
00:03:44,315 --> 00:03:48,253
Now mistake number one, and it's the most common error in web scraping.

77
00:03:48,253 --> 00:03:51,470
I typed dot prices, with an s, a class that doesn't exist.

78
00:03:51,470 --> 00:03:51,858
Run it.

79
00:03:51,858 --> 00:03:52,746
Attribute error.

80
00:03:52,746 --> 00:03:55,131
None type object has no attribute get text.

81
00:03:55,131 --> 00:03:58,903
Select one found nothing, so it returned None, and None has no text.

82
00:03:58,903 --> 00:04:01,343
When you see None type, check your selector.

83
00:04:01,343 --> 00:04:03,673
Copy the class name straight from Inspect.

84
00:04:04,373 --> 00:04:04,872
Step four.

85
00:04:04,872 --> 00:04:06,321
A table needs more than text.

86
00:04:06,321 --> 00:04:07,120
Here's one book.

87
00:04:07,120 --> 00:04:09,518
For the link, select the a tag inside the title.

88
00:04:09,518 --> 00:04:10,767
Get text gives its words.

89
00:04:10,767 --> 00:04:13,315
Square brackets with h r e f give the link address.

90
00:04:13,315 --> 00:04:16,013
But it's only half an address, it starts with a slash.

91
00:04:16,013 --> 00:04:19,110
Url join glues it to the page address, so you get a full link.

92
00:04:19,110 --> 00:04:21,558
Square brackets also read data stars, the rating.

93
00:04:21,558 --> 00:04:26,653
And for the price, strip the dollar sign and turn it into a float, a real number you can sort and add.

94
00:04:27,353 --> 00:04:28,407
The real output.

95
00:04:28,407 --> 00:04:29,066
The title.

96
00:04:29,066 --> 00:04:30,054
The short link.

97
00:04:30,054 --> 00:04:31,898
The full link from url join.

98
00:04:31,898 --> 00:04:32,556
Two stars.

99
00:04:32,556 --> 00:04:35,981
And fourteen point nine nine, as a number, not text.

100
00:04:36,681 --> 00:04:37,752
Mistake number two.

101
00:04:37,752 --> 00:04:41,641
There are only three pages, but what if your loop asks for page four?

102
00:04:41,641 --> 00:04:42,937
Requests does not stop.

103
00:04:42,937 --> 00:04:48,405
It quietly returns status four oh four, not found, and your script carries on with an error page.

104
00:04:48,405 --> 00:04:49,532
The fix is one line.

105
00:04:49,532 --> 00:04:50,490
Raise for status.

106
00:04:50,490 --> 00:04:55,676
If the status is an error, it stops right there, and tells you exactly which address failed.

107
00:04:56,376 --> 00:04:58,957
Mistake number three is the confusing one.

108
00:04:58,957 --> 00:05:02,520
In my browser, the bottom of the page shows three reviews.

109
00:05:02,520 --> 00:05:06,330
But Python finds zero, and the box just says, loading reviews.

110
00:05:06,330 --> 00:05:06,575
Why?

111
00:05:06,575 --> 00:05:10,139
The reviews are added by JavaScript, after the page loads.

112
00:05:10,139 --> 00:05:12,228
Your browser runs that JavaScript.

113
00:05:12,228 --> 00:05:13,334
Requests does not.

114
00:05:13,334 --> 00:05:15,115
It only gets the raw H T M L.

115
00:05:15,815 --> 00:05:16,923
Here's the proof.

116
00:05:16,923 --> 00:05:20,309
Zero reviews found, and the text is loading reviews.

117
00:05:20,309 --> 00:05:21,481
Nothing is broken.

118
00:05:21,481 --> 00:05:24,997
That data simply isn't in the file requests downloads.

119
00:05:25,697 --> 00:05:26,152
The fix?

120
00:05:26,152 --> 00:05:30,753
Open the developer tools again, and look at the Network tab while the page loads.

121
00:05:30,753 --> 00:05:33,991
You'll often find the data file the JavaScript downloads.

122
00:05:33,991 --> 00:05:35,695
Here it's reviews dot j s o n.

123
00:05:35,695 --> 00:05:39,785
Ask for that file directly, and dot j s o n turns it into a Python list.

124
00:05:39,785 --> 00:05:42,058
Three reviews, clean, no H T M L at all.

125
00:05:42,058 --> 00:05:49,045
If there's no such file, a browser tool like Playwright can run the JavaScript for you, but that's a topic for another day.

126
00:05:49,745 --> 00:05:51,300
Step five, the full scraper.

127
00:05:51,300 --> 00:05:57,019
The address has a page number, so base is the address with curly braces, and format puts the number in.

128
00:05:57,019 --> 00:05:58,962
A for loop runs pages one to three.

129
00:05:58,962 --> 00:06:02,349
For each page, download, raise for status, and make the soup.

130
00:06:02,349 --> 00:06:05,459
Then, for every book, add a dictionary to the rows list.

131
00:06:05,459 --> 00:06:06,514
One key per column.

132
00:06:06,514 --> 00:06:09,679
Title, author, price, stars, in stock, and the full link.

133
00:06:10,379 --> 00:06:11,824
Two details matter here.

134
00:06:11,824 --> 00:06:14,835
Time dot sleep one waits one second between pages.

135
00:06:14,835 --> 00:06:17,846
That's being polite, so you never flood a website.

136
00:06:17,846 --> 00:06:23,628
And at the end, pandas turns the list of rows into a table, a data frame, and to c s v saves it.

137
00:06:23,628 --> 00:06:27,241
Index equals false keeps pandas row numbers out of the file.

138
00:06:27,941 --> 00:06:28,429
Run it.

139
00:06:28,429 --> 00:06:29,962
Page one, eight books.

140
00:06:29,962 --> 00:06:31,216
Page two, sixteen.

141
00:06:31,216 --> 00:06:32,889
Page three, twenty four.

142
00:06:32,889 --> 00:06:35,886
Saved twenty four books to books dot c s v.

143
00:06:36,586 --> 00:06:37,813
And here's the file.

144
00:06:37,813 --> 00:06:40,144
A header line, then one line per book.

145
00:06:40,144 --> 00:06:45,359
Title, author, price as a number, stars, true or false for in stock, and a full link.

146
00:06:45,359 --> 00:06:48,733
Double click it and it opens in Excel or Google Sheets.

147
00:06:49,433 --> 00:06:52,133
Here's the whole scrape on the real page one.

148
00:06:52,133 --> 00:06:59,632
Every book lights up, and its row lands in the table, with the price as a number and the stars from the data stars attribute.

149
00:06:59,632 --> 00:07:02,092
That's exactly what ended up in the file.

150
00:07:02,792 --> 00:07:03,787
Now the fun part.

151
00:07:03,787 --> 00:07:05,309
Your data is a real table.

152
00:07:05,309 --> 00:07:11,279
Read it back with pandas, keep the books with four stars or more that are in stock, and sort by price.

153
00:07:11,279 --> 00:07:14,088
Five cheap, well rated books, found in a second.

154
00:07:14,088 --> 00:07:16,663
And the average price, thirteen ninety five.

155
00:07:16,663 --> 00:07:19,355
Try doing that by scrolling through a website.

156
00:07:20,055 --> 00:07:22,683
If your scraper fails, it's usually one of these.

157
00:07:22,683 --> 00:07:27,725
None type has no attribute means a selector found nothing, so check the class name in Inspect.

158
00:07:27,725 --> 00:07:32,176
A four oh three or four oh four error means the page refused you, or doesn't exist.

159
00:07:32,176 --> 00:07:33,410
Read the error address.

160
00:07:33,410 --> 00:07:40,757
And if you get empty results while the browser shows data, that data comes from JavaScript, so look for the data file in the Network tab.

161
00:07:41,457 --> 00:07:41,924
Pro tip.

162
00:07:41,924 --> 00:07:44,372
Python can check robots dot t x t for you.

163
00:07:44,372 --> 00:07:46,470
Robot file parser comes with Python.

164
00:07:46,470 --> 00:07:48,860
Give it the robots address and call read.

165
00:07:48,860 --> 00:07:51,541
Then can fetch tells you if a page is allowed.

166
00:07:51,541 --> 00:07:52,590
The catalog, true.

167
00:07:52,590 --> 00:07:53,989
The admin folder, false.

168
00:07:53,989 --> 00:07:57,486
And crawl delay says how many seconds to wait between pages.

169
00:07:57,486 --> 00:08:00,517
Here it's one, which is exactly what our sleep does.

170
00:08:01,217 --> 00:08:02,076
And the speed.

171
00:08:02,076 --> 00:08:05,756
Three pages, twenty four books, in three and a half seconds.

172
00:08:05,756 --> 00:08:09,988
And three of those seconds are the polite pauses we added on purpose.

173
00:08:09,988 --> 00:08:11,828
The work itself takes a blink.

174
00:08:12,528 --> 00:08:13,930
Here's your cheat sheet.

175
00:08:13,930 --> 00:08:15,799
Requests dot get with a timeout.

176
00:08:15,799 --> 00:08:16,792
Raise for status.

177
00:08:16,792 --> 00:08:19,070
Beautiful Soup with the h t m l parser.

178
00:08:19,070 --> 00:08:21,932
Select for all matches, select one for the first.

179
00:08:21,932 --> 00:08:23,100
Get text with strip.

180
00:08:23,100 --> 00:08:24,911
Square brackets for attributes.

181
00:08:24,911 --> 00:08:26,020
Url join for links.

182
00:08:26,020 --> 00:08:27,188
Sleep between pages.

183
00:08:27,188 --> 00:08:28,415
And to c s v to save.

184
00:08:28,415 --> 00:08:29,817
Pause and screenshot it.

185
00:08:30,517 --> 00:08:31,208
Quick recap.

186
00:08:31,208 --> 00:08:35,184
Download the page with requests, with a timeout and raise for status.

187
00:08:35,184 --> 00:08:35,991
Make the soup.

188
00:08:35,991 --> 00:08:37,547
Find your map with Inspect.

189
00:08:37,547 --> 00:08:41,062
Select every item, and pull out text, attributes and numbers.

190
00:08:41,062 --> 00:08:42,676
Loop over the pages, slowly.

191
00:08:42,676 --> 00:08:43,828
Save it with pandas.

192
00:08:43,828 --> 00:08:45,672
And always scrape the right way.

193
00:08:46,372 --> 00:08:54,986
Next on Shortcut Lab, we'll take the data you just scraped and send it somewhere useful, with an automatic email report written in Python.

194
00:08:54,986 --> 00:08:56,983
Subscribe, so you don't miss it.
